  "id": "Compound::DB00402 - Gene::2556",
  "verbalization": "Eszopiclone, a central nervous system depressant, is shown to interact with Gabra4 and Gabra2, which are subunits of GABA-A receptors. These receptors are critical for modulating neuronal activity and are involved in benzodiazepine receptor function. The evidence suggests that Eszopiclone binds to Gabra4, which in turn participates in benzodiazepine receptor activity, and similarly, Gabra2 participates in benzodiazepine receptor activity. This implies that Eszopiclone may influence GABA-A receptor function through interactions with Gabra4 or Gabra2. The shared involvement of these receptors in benzodiazepine receptor activity suggests a potential mechanism by which Eszopiclone modulates GABA-A receptor function, possibly through a common pathway. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of Gabra3 as a downstream target, possibly through the activation of GABA-A receptors, and underscores the importance of GABA-A receptor subunits in the pharmacological effects of Eszopiclone.",
